{{also|layoff|lay-off}}
==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{audio|en|En-au-lay off.ogg|a=AU}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|lay<,,laid> off}}

# {{lb|en|transitive|chiefly|US|idiom}} (of an employer) To [[dismiss]] ([[worker]]s) from employment, [[e.g.]] at a time of low [[business]] [[volume]] or through no [[fault]] of the worker, often with a [[w:Severance package|severance package]].
# {{lb|en|transitive|of a bookmaker}} To [[place]] all or part of a [[bet]] with another [[bookmaker]] in order to [[reduce]] [[risk]].
#: {{syn|en|hedge one's bets}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|idiomatic}} To [[cease]], [[quit]], [[stop]] (doing something).
#: {{ux|en|'''Lay off''' the singing, will you! I'm trying to study.}}
#: {{ux|en|When are you gonna '''lay off''' smoking?}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|and|intransitive|idiomatic}} To stop [[bother]]ing, [[tease|teasing]], or [[pester]]ing someone; to leave (someone) alone.
#: {{ux|en|Just '''lay off''', okay! I've had enough!}}
#: {{ux|en|Things have been better since the boss has been '''laying off''' a little.}}
#: {{ux|en|I told him to '''lay off''' me but he wouldn't stop.}}
#: {{ux|en|'''Lay off''' it, already!}}
# {{lb|en|ambitransitive|art}} In [[painting]], to apply gentle strokes to smooth a wet coat of paint so as to remove visible roller- or brush-marks, commonly using a dry brush; a similar technique, but using a loaded laying-off brush, may produce a smooth coat of paint when using a roller or the usual brush techniques would leave marks.
#: {{syn|en|tip off}}
#: ''At any [[pro]] paint shop ask for '''laying off''' brushes. These are [[natural]] [[bristle]], wide, thin brushes designed for [[tip off|tipping off]], not for holding a paint load.'' (Sourced from a web forum exchange)
#: ''He shows me how to '''lay off''' the paint — and moves his paintbrush across the section he had already painted, again and again.''
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To plan out (a navigational course) using a chart.

====Usage notes====
* In the first two transitive senses the object can come before or after the particle (''laid off the whole department''). If the object is a pronoun, then it must come before the particle (''laid them off'').
* In the two idiomatic "cease" senses, all objects, including pronouns, come after the complete phrase (''lay off me''!). Also, in this sense the verb cannot be used in the passive voice; for example, ''Finally, the bullies laid off the victim'' cannot be rephrased as, *''Finally, the victim was laid off by the bullies''.

